    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and orphaned young, Muhammad reported in about 610 that he had received the first of the revelations that Muslims hold to be the word of God, and began preaching publicly in 613.</li><li>After 13 years of persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> he and his followers migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 622, the event that began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>, and there he led a community that grew through treaties, raids and wars.</li><li>By his death on 8 June 632 most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and the dispute over who should succeed him shaped the later division between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia Muslims.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> contains comparatively little direct information about Muhammad himself, although it names him in several verses and briefly refers to episodes such as the migration to Yathrib and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-badr">Battle of Badr</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

