    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 570</span><span class="rf-text">Muhammad is born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a> clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 610</span><span class="rf-text">He reports a visit from Gabriel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-nour">cave of Hira</a> and the first revelation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">613</span><span class="rf-text">He begins to preach publicly</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">622</span><span class="rf-text">The Hijrah: he and his followers migrate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">624</span><span class="rf-text">The Muslims win the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-badr">Battle of Badr</a> against a larger Meccan force</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">628</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hudaybiyyah">Treaty of Hudaybiyya</a> establishes a ten-year truce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">630</span><span class="rf-text">He marches on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> with 10,000 men and takes the city with minimal casualties</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">632</span><span class="rf-text">He leads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farewell-pilgrimage">Farewell Pilgrimage</a> and dies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> on 8 June</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/black-stone"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-stone.jpg" alt="Black Stone" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_stone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to the traditional account, Muhammad was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> around 570 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a> clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, then a dominant force in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. His father, Abdullah, died almost six months before he was born, and his mother, Amina, died when he was six. He was cared for by his grandfather Abd al-Muttalib until the latter's death, then by his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-talib-ibn-abd-al-muttalib">Abu Talib</a>, the new leader of the clan, and he later led caravans on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> for another uncle, Abbas.</p><p>The historical record of Mecca in these years is limited and fragmentary, which makes it hard to separate fact from legend. Several Islamic narratives tell of a boyhood journey to Syria and a meeting with the monk Bahira, who foretold his prophethood; modern historians, and some medieval Muslim scholars such as al-Dhahabi, have judged all accounts of Bahira to be fictitious. At about 25 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khadija-bint-khuwaylid">Khadija</a>, a wealthy merchant of about 40 who had hired him to take a caravan to Syria, and he remained monogamous until her death. In 605, by a narrative collected by Ibn Ishaq, he settled a dispute among the clans over who should set the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a> back into the rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Muhammad&#8221; cites where it covers Early biographies, Early life:</p><ul><li>F. Buhl, Muḥammad (Brill, 1993)</li><li>W. Montgomery Watt, Muhammad: Prophet and Statesman (Oxford University Press, 1961)</li><li>Barbara Roggema, The Legend of Sergius Baḥīrā: Eastern Christian Apologetics and Apocalyptic in Response to Islam (Brill, 2008)</li><li>W. Montgomery Watt, Muhammad at Mecca (Oxford University Press, 1953)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/khadija-bint-khuwaylid">Khadija bint Khuwaylid<span>Married</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha<span>Married</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-nour"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jabal-al-nour.jpg" alt="Jabal al-Nour" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-nour">Jabal al-Nour</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jabbal_An-Nour_(2024).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kaliper1, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to Islamic tradition, in 610, when he was 40, the angel Gabriel appeared to Muhammad in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-nour">cave of Hira</a>, where he sometimes withdrew for prayer, and instructed him to read; the first verses delivered are held to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> 96:1-5. The traditional account says he came down from the mountain frightened and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khadija-bint-khuwaylid">Khadija</a> reassured him, as did her Christian cousin Waraqah ibn Nawfal. Muslim tradition holds that Khadija was the first to believe, followed by his ten-year-old cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, his friend Abu Bakr and his adopted son Zayd.</p><p>Muhammad was at first reluctant to speak of the revelations and confided in a few relatives and friends. Around 613 he began to preach publicly, proclaiming that God is One, that complete submission (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>) to God is the right way of life, and that he was a prophet and messenger of God. Many early followers were women, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>, servants and slaves, who memorised each new revelation as he recited it. He introduced prayer with physical postures of submission and almsgiving (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a>) as a duty of the community (ummah). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> at first took little notice, but tensions grew once he attacked their beliefs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kaaba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaaba.jpg" alt="Kaaba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ka%27ba,_Great_Mosque_of_Mecca,_Saudi_Arabia_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 622, after pledges at Aqaba from men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, Muhammad called on the Meccan Muslims to move to Medina, and arrived there with Abu Bakr on 4 September 622. The Hijrah marks the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>. In Medina the Muslims built a mosque that served as his residence and a place for prayer, public meetings and the care of the poor, and Bilal, a former Abyssinian slave, was chosen to call the community to prayer. Muhammad also set down the text commonly called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-medina">Constitution of Medina</a>, in which the Arab and Jewish tribes of the oasis promised to live in peace with the Muslims and to defend Medina if attacked; scholars dispute whether it was a treaty or a unilateral proclamation.</p><p>War with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> followed. At Badr in March 624 some three hundred Muslims defeated a larger Meccan force, killing at least forty-five Meccans for the loss of fourteen. The Muslims were beaten at Uhud in 625 and withstood a siege of Medina, behind a trench, in 627. In 628 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hudaybiyyah">Treaty of Hudaybiyya</a> set a ten-year truce, and in 630 Muhammad marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> with 10,000 men, took the city with minimal casualties, declared an amnesty with a few exceptions and destroyed the statues of the Arabian gods around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>. In 632 he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farewell-pilgrimage">Farewell Pilgrimage</a> and preached a sermon.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Muhammad&#8221; cites where it covers Conquest of Mecca, Islamic social reforms:</p><ul><li>John Esposito, Islam: The Straight Path (Oxford University Press, 1998)</li><li>P. M. Holt, The Cambridge History of Islam (Cambridge University Press, 1977)</li><li>W. Montgomery Watt, Muhammad at Medina (Oxford University Press, 1956)</li><li>Alfred Guillaume, The Life of Muhammad: A Translation of Ishaq's &quot;Sirat Rasul Allah&quot; (Oxford University Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench.jpg" alt="Battle of the Trench" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench">Battle of the Trench</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Trench_627_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DrZubairRashid, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, Muhammad's opponents were chiefly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> leaders, who said he had derided their ancestors and gods and divided their community. Contemporaries also accused him of being possessed by a jinn, a soothsayer or a magician. His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-talib-ibn-abd-al-muttalib">Abu Talib</a> protected him but did not accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. In 616 the other Quraysh clans agreed to a ban on trade and marriage with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a>, lifted in 619. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khadija-bint-khuwaylid">Khadija</a> and Abu Talib died in 619, he sought support in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a>, where, according to the account, he was rejected and stoned.</p><p>The Medinan years are told with episodes of violence that sources and scholars treat differently. The Banu Qaynuqa and the Banu Nadir were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> after sieges, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench">Battle of the Trench</a> the Banu Qurayza surrendered. By Ibn Ishaq's account the men were beheaded and the women and children enslaved, with the judgement given by Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, whom Muhammad approved; Walid N. Arafat and Barakat Ahmad dispute that account, and Meir J. Kister has argued against them in turn. Criticism of him has existed since the 7th century and now questions his legitimacy as a prophet, his conduct, his marriages, his ownership of slaves and his treatment of enemies; Western and Byzantine Christians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> called him a false prophet.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Muhammad&#8221; cites where it covers Opposition in Mecca, Beginning of armed conflict, Conflicts with Jewish tribes, Criticism:</p><ul><li>F. Buhl, Muḥammad (Brill, 1993)</li><li>Jonathan E. Brockopp, The Cambridge Companion to Muhammad (Cambridge University Press, 2010)</li><li>P. M. Holt, The Cambridge History of Islam (Cambridge University Press, 1977)</li><li>W. Montgomery Watt, Muhammad: Prophet and Statesman (Oxford University Press, 1961)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque.jpg" alt="Prophet's Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madeena_masjid_nabavi_12122008230.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bluemangoa2z at Malayalam Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In June 632, after praying at a burial site, Muhammad suffered a severe headache. He spent his illness moving among his wives' rooms, and asked to stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>'s, walking there with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali</a> and Fadl ibn Abbas. According to various sources, including Sahih al-Bukhari, he said he felt his aorta being severed because of the food he had eaten at Khaybar, where a meal was said to have been poisoned. He died on 8 June 632, and his last words are reported as a prayer: "O God, forgive me and have mercy on me; and let me join the highest companions." The historian Alfred T. Welch, who presumes the illness to have been an ordinary Medinan fever, limits his speculation to physical and mental fatigue.</p><p>He was buried where he died, in Aisha's house, which was later included in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a>. The question of a successor divided the community at once. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a> nominated Abu Bakr, who was confirmed as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. Some companions disputed this choice, holding that Muhammad had designated his cousin and son-in-law Ali ibn Abi Talib as successor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghadir-khumm">Ghadir Khumm</a>. This dispute is the origin of the later division between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia. Within a decade the Muslims had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Muhammad&#8221; cites where it covers Subduing the Hawazin and Thaqif and the expedition to Tabuk, Farewell pilgrimage, Death, Succession:</p><ul><li>Richard A. Gabriel, Muhammad: Islam's First Great General (University of Oklahoma Press, 2007)</li><li>Richard A. Gabriel, Muhammad: Islam's First Great General (University of Oklahoma Press, 2014)</li><li>Russ Rodgers, The Generalship of Muhammad: Battles and Campaigns of the Prophet of Allah (University Press of Florida, 2012)</li><li>P. M. Holt, The Cambridge History of Islam (Cambridge University Press, 1977)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/quran"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quran.jpg" alt="Quran" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birmingham_Quran_manuscript_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In Islamic belief Muhammad is the last prophet sent by God, and the Shahada declares that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is a Messenger of God. The revelations he reported form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, which Muslims regard as the verbatim word of God. His teachings and practices, preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> and in his biography (sirah), form the sunnah, a model for Muslims and a source of Islamic law; many details of the daily prayers, the fast and the pilgrimage are found only there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders trace their spiritual descent to him, and his birthday is celebrated as a major feast in much of the Muslim world. Muslims commonly follow his name with a blessing, and generally avoid depicting him, instead using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> such as the hilya.</p><p>He is also honoured in other faiths: as one of the seven main prophets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> faith, as a Manifestation of God in the Baha'i Faith, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> as one of the divine messengers. Historians depend on sources written in the 8th and 9th centuries, and John Burton judged that little of use can be recovered of the early life. Watt and Bernard Lewis rejected the idea that he was a deliberate impostor, and Lewis held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> denounced aristocratic privilege and opened careers to talent. European writers from Postel to Carlyle assessed him very differently, from sage lawgiver to, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>'s play, a symbol of fanaticism.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Muhammad&#8221; cites where it covers Islamic tradition, European appreciation, Other religions:</p><ul><li>Jonathan E. Brockopp, The Cambridge Companion to Muhammad (Cambridge University Press, 2010)</li><li>F. Buhl, Muḥammad (Brill, 1993)</li><li>Richard A. Gabriel, Muhammad: Islam's First Great General (University of Oklahoma Press, 2014)</li><li>Francis Edward Peters, Jesus and Muhammad: Parallel Tracks, Parallel Lives (Oxford University Press, 2010)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">On the globe, stop by stop</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/journeys?j=hijra">The Hijrah: From Mecca to Medina<span>Journey</span></a></p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/tala-al-badru-alayna">Tala al-Badru Alayna<span>c. 622</span></a><a href="/songs/al-burda">Al-Burda<span>c. 1250</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Muhammad (c. 570 to 632) was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, reported revelations from about 610 and led his followers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 622. By his death most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> had accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Muslims hold him to be the final prophet; historians study him through sources written later.</em></p>

